<h2>Why I started</h2>
<p>I worked in telecom for ten years: first as an engineer measuring and tuning LTE quality, then selling 5G base station equipment. I know how carrier gear ends up on apartment rooftops and which sites a carrier can't afford to lose. So I tried a data business in a field I actually know.</p>
<p>The two ideas right before this one died because their markets were too small. This time I measured the market first. Cell site rent in Korea is a ₩451.9 billion a year market, and in 2024 the three carriers were fined about ₩20 billion for colluding on rents. Yet nobody told building owners what the going rate was.</p>

<h2>What I built</h2>
<p>Korean apartment complexes must publish their audit reports. I opened 8 of them and 4 listed lease contracts per carrier. A random sample of 200 showed contracts in 27%. On one rooftop, KT paid ₩5.9 million while SK paid ₩0.9 million.</p>
<p>By the second night there was a national rate table. I read the reports of 22,543 complexes and pulled 10,360 contracts from 4,418 of them. The median rooftop rent from one carrier was ₩3.3 million a year, about ₩4 million around Seoul and about ₩2 million in smaller cities. Matching it against radio station permits showed that in 4 of 5 complexes with auto-renewed contracts, the rent stayed the same while the equipment grew.</p>

<h2>Where it got stuck</h2>
<p>I went to meet the first potential customer in person: a building manager at an apartment complex in Jeju. When I showed the numbers, this is what I heard: "We've never renegotiated." "We don't even have the contract." And the decisive one: "I'm on a salary, and I might end up being the one who pays for it."</p>
<p>A manager gets nothing if the rent goes up, and takes the blame if the negotiation goes wrong. What they wanted wasn't market rates. "If someone takes the hit and makes a court precedent, then we'll ask for the same." They wanted grounds for the right to be paid: case law.</p>
<p>Three of the hypotheses I wrote down beforehand held. Carriers set the price, complexes don't know each other's rates, and rent doesn't move when equipment grows. The one that failed was that an extra million won a year would get people moving. The people who gain (residents) and the person who has to act (the manager) were different. I judged it not viable and dropped it.</p>

<h2>What a human had to do</h2>
<p>I called the manager, set up the visit and showed up with a box of energy drinks. The hypothesis that equipment grows while rent stays flat was mine, and so was the call to stop. Reading 22,543 reports was the AI's job.</p>

<h2>What I learned</h2>
<p>4.5 hours of work over six days. The data came fast and it was accurate. What broke wasn't the data, it was the buyer. If the person who benefits isn't the person who has to act, it doesn't sell. This was the second idea, after public tenders, to break in exactly that shape. And it happened in a field I know well.</p>
